ong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Greenville-Spartanburg has nearly stopped setting record lows","p_rec":"If Greenville-Spartanburg's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Greenville-Spartanburg has set <strong class=\"sig\">18 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 71 that chance alone would give, and <strong>90 record highs</strong>, more than the 71 that chance alone would give. The 2010s hold the most record highs of any decade (38). Greenville-Spartanburg's all-time high is 107\u00b0F, on July 1, 2012.","t_chg":"Greenville-Spartanburg has warmed, days and nights alike","p_chg":"Across 130 complete years: Greenville-Spartanburg's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.17\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.09 to +0.26), about +2.2\u00b0F since 1896. Greenville-Spartanburg's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.17\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.12 to +0.23), about +2.2\u00b0F since 1896.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 11 of 12 months. Highs rose in 6 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Greenville-Spartanburg's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (\u22120.14 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 49.6 inches a year. The wettest year was 2020 (73.70 in); the driest, 2007 (31.08 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 11 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}